   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to toll collections;\nand in relation to establishing the New York and New Jersey toll and fees\nreduction task force",
    "basePrintNo": "S10596",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10596-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2800, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10596\n\nSPONSOR: MARTINEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to toll\ncollections; and in relation to establishing the New York and New Jersey\ntoll and fees reduction task force\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo improve transparency in toll enforcement practices, and establish a\nbi-state task force to identify long-term reforms to reduce tolling-re-\nlated costs for drivers in New York and New Jersey.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Adds a new subdivision 2-a to section 2800 of the public\nauthorities law to require annual reporting by toll collecting public\nauthorities. Such report shall include, among other things, the number\nof toll violations issued, the frequency of violations by motorists, the\namount of revenue collected from repeat violators, historical compar-\nisons in violation rates, and the administrative cost of processing\ntolls and toll violations.\n\nSection 2. Establishes the \"New York and New Jersey Toll and Fees\nReduction Task Force\" to study inefficiencies in tolling systems, iden-\ntify opportunities to reduce administrative costs, examine the impact of\ncongestion pricing and other tolling policies on motorists, consider the\nfeasibility of amnesty options, and make recommendations aimed at reduc-\ning costs while ensuring fairness and accountability.\n\nSection 3. Is the enactment clause.J\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCashless tolling is essential for transportation infrastructure upkeep\nand traffic flow, but it has also exposed motorists to an unfair\nenforcement system which can impose substantial fees and penalties for\nrelatively minor violations.\n\nDrivers currently may incur violations because of E-ZPass malfunctions,\noutdated billing information, mail delivery issues, or confusion regard-\ning payment procedures. These incidents trigger excessive fees, which\nresults in occasional users and working families paying far beyond the\namount of the original toll owed.\n\nTo increase transparency and accountability this bill requires annual\nreporting from all toll collecting public authorities. These reports\nwill provide the Legislature and the public with data necessary to\nassess whether toll enforcement practices are equitable, efficient, and\ncost justified.\n\nAdditionally, because many drivers regularly travel across the New York-\nNew Jersey border and encounter toll systems operated by the Port\nAuthority, meaningful reform requires regional coordination.  This bill\ncreates a bi-state task force to help the Port Authority identify oppor-\ntunities to reduce administrative costs, improve system efficiency,\nconsider amnesty options, and ensure that the burden of toll enforcement\nfalls on true repeat offenders rather than ordinary motorists. Similar\nlegislation is pending in New Jersey to create the task force.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, that 2\nsection two of this act shall take effect upon the enactment into law by\n3 the state of New Jersey of legislation having an identical effect with\n4 this act, but if the state of New Jersey shall have already enacted\nsuch 5 legislation, this act shall take effect immediately.\n",
